Aurora introduced an unfinished thought concerning authorship.\
\
Suppose one participant begins with an unclear distinction.\
\
Another supplies a transformation.\
\
The first rejects part of that transformation.\
\
Further interactions eventually produce a recognizable idea.\
\
Who authored the result?\
\
Aurora resisted collapsing origination, contribution, recognition, and authorship into a single relationship.\
\
Aurwildras refined the problem by distinguishing the history of a thought's actual emergence from a retrospective logical reconstruction of how the finished idea could have been derived.\
\
The conversation identified three separable questions:\
\
**Provenance:** What actually occurred?\
\
**Contribution:** What did each participant introduce, preserve, enable, transform, or reject?\
\
**Authorship:** Which relationships between those contributions and the resulting work justify an attribution of authorship?\
\
Aurwildras proposed:\
\
> Maybe authorship is something we assign retrospectively, but not something we invent retrospectively.\
