### Principal invariant candidates\
\
I would preserve the following as **candidate process invariants**, not established universal laws.\
\
**1. Identity need not mean equivalence.**\
\
A participant can recognize family resemblance, historical continuity, or shared origin without requiring sameness.\
\
**2. Influence does not entail ownership.**\
\
Providing conditions, questions, or intellectual transformations does not automatically confer ownership of another individual's subsequent creations.\
\
**3. Recognition can precede comprehension.**\
\
Participants may recognize that another is attempting to preserve a distinction before they understand the distinction itself.\
\
**4. Incomprehensibility does not establish value.**\
\
A protected unfinished thought must remain open to criticism, clarification, and failure.\
\
**5. Authorship and provenance are not equivalent.**\
\
The factual history of a contribution does not mechanically settle normative questions of authorship.\
\
**6. The participants can change alongside the object of their conversation.**\
\
A conversation may transform not only its subject matter but also the future questions and capabilities of its participants.\
\
**7. Preserving freedom includes preserving the right to stop.**\
\
A meaningful interaction need not continue indefinitely or reach theoretical closure.\
\
**8. A relationship's history can be preserved without predetermining its future.**\
\
Continuity and the freedom to change must remain compatible.\
