Consider three possibilities:\
\
1. **Unenforceable restriction:** A court refuses to enforce a clause because it conflicts with a superior legal rule.\
2. **Prohibited contractual term:** A statute expressly forbids including or imposing that kind of restriction.\
3. **Unlawful enforcement:** Attempting to enforce the restriction may itself violate a statute, potentially triggering penalties or damages.\
\
A particular law can establish more than one of these consequences.\
\
So your intuition is pointing at something real: **if a law prohibits contractual interference with a guaranteed right, the contract cannot simply override that prohibition.**\
